## GTK 4 — LGPL-2.1

The binary links GTK 4 dynamically against whatever GTK 4 the user's system
provides. It does not vendor, statically link, or patch GTK 4.

The LGPL requires that anyone receiving a copy of the binary may replace the
GTK 4 shared libraries it uses with modified versions, and that the
corresponding GTK 4 sources be available to them. Dynamic linking against the
system library satisfies the first of those provided the second holds, which on
most distributions it does — the GTK 4 sources ship in the development package:

| Distribution | GTK 4 source package |
| --- | --- |
| Arch | `gtk4` (the `-dbg`/`gtk4-devel` variants where split) |
| Fedora | `gtk4-devel` |
| Debian, Ubuntu | `libgtk-4-dev` |
| openSUSE | `gtk4-devel` |

Building from this repository with `cargo install --path .` produces the same
binary, with GTK 4 again supplied by the build host's package manager, so a
distro package that builds from source and depends on its own `gtk4` is
carrying a binary that is straightforwardly LGPL-compliant.

What would *not* be compliant, and is not done here: statically linking GTK 4
into the binary, patching GTK 4, or shipping a build with the relinking
ability removed.

## Tokyonight Night — MIT

The default colour palette in `data/style.css` is Tokyonight Night, by Brandon
Sayer, distributed under the MIT licence:

    https://github.com/enkia/tokyonight

The palette values are used as written. No Tokyonight source code, theme file,
or stylesheet is vendored into this repository: `data/style.css` is this
project's own file, and replacing its colours replaces the palette. Tokyonight
is therefore an attribution rather than a dependency — nothing here requires it
to be installed, and the notepad renders the same with any palette at all.
